const questions=["Which objects and details make the soldiers' poverty concrete rather than abstract?","How does the relationship between the trader and the commander influence Fierro's account of injustice?","What is the significance of Fierro having served yet being excluded from the payroll list?","Does the narrator claim to know exactly who kept the money? Identify what he witnessed and what he suspects."];
const intro={"n":4,"title":"Unpaid Soldiers, Poverty, and the Fortín","summary":"Fierro recounts the extreme poverty of the men stationed at the frontier post: unpaid wages, worn-out clothes and the loss of personal belongings. He describes a trader linked to the commander who exchanges yerba and tobacco for goods, then collects debts when some soldiers finally receive money. Fierro himself receives nothing. An officer says his name was not on the payroll, despite the time he says he has spent serving. The canto portrays a system in which military authority and commerce can exploit the same men.","people":"Martín Fierro as narrator; fellow soldiers; a pulpero who trades with the men; the mayor and commander who control payment and complaints.","themes":"Unpaid labor and bureaucratic exclusion; frontier deprivation; trade and debt; unequal authority; satire and the narrator's resentment.","words":"fortín (small frontier military outpost); pulpero (rural trader); boliche (small shop or drinking establishment); cobres (coins or money); apero (horse-riding equipment); reyuno (a government-owned or military horse in this context).","questions":["Which objects and details make the soldiers' poverty concrete rather than abstract?","How does the relationship between the trader and the commander influence Fierro's account of injustice?","What is the significance of Fierro having served yet being excluded from the payroll list?","Does the narrator claim to know exactly who kept the money? Identify what he witnessed and what he suspects."],"nums":[105,115,126],"analysis":"The point is not merely that Fierro dislikes military life. The unpaid salary and trading arrangements reveal how he experiences the fort as a place where officials can control both his labor and his access to necessities. Notice how the poem moves from bodily suffering to accounts and lists: administrative records become instruments of power. Because this is Fierro's testimony, distinguish what the canto explicitly reports from any unproven explanation of where the money went.","related":"/en/magazine/frontier-forts/","relatedTitle":"Life in frontier forts"};
